From 99670201b6bae5cd6ad2dee7d6e92cb8c0a00918 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 00:46:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] fix: gate B compares the best constant against per-state,
 not against the baseline

My criterion was wrong and it cost the lower window a stop. T11 section 3 said
any fully covered constant matching or beating the baseline's 32/48 stops the
round. But the baseline IS a constant -- T = 0 -- so "some constant matches the
baseline" is a tautology, and the rule fires whenever another constant happens
to tie while saying nothing about whether a constant captures the headroom.

What gate B actually asks is whether a constant reaches what choosing per state
reaches. On the 48-seed block the best constant is T = 0 at 32/48 and the
per-state choice is 40/48, a gap of eight seeds, so gate B passes. The constant
that tripped the old rule, T = 20, only ties on completion: 98.6 s against
78.8 s, 277.0 N s against 187.7, 23 illegal crossings against 18, and the four
seeds it rescues are not the four it breaks.

The report now prints the best constant, the per-state result and the gap, and
carries mean time and mean fuel per row so a row that ties on count while
losing everywhere else cannot be read as sufficient.

Also records what the per-state choice costs: it picks T = 0 on all 32
baseline-completing seeds, so it is the fixed setpoint there bitwise, and mean
fuel rises 187.7 to 230.9 N s purely from the eight episodes that previously
finished nowhere.

